From: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/9] SPARC64 firmware-call and panic preparation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

These changes separate generic PROM console and panic handling from the
PRIMEPOWER platform policy. They handle partial and stalled writes,
avoid waiting for the PROM buffer during panic, capture secondary CPUs
before orderly reboot, and prepare checked firmware-service wrappers.

The direct wrapper still succeeds on existing platforms. Returning status
lets a platform refuse entry without callers consuming unchanged result
cells. That API preparation is separate from the PRIMEPOWER admission
policy. A firmware call or CPU capture already in progress is not bounded.

These nine patches apply together to vanilla v7.3-rc1 without M3000 or
PRIMEPOWER support. The exact cleaned integration passed PRIMEPOWER
UP/SMP boot, serial and firmware-entry checks, plus Ultra45 UP and
eight-vCPU T5120 LDOM boot/reboot, fork/COW and disk/network checks.
Ultra45 also passed native serial and BREAK/go. The guest's eight strands
share one physical core and use virtual I/O; native sun4v PCI and
cross-core operation were not tested in this qualification.
The M3000 IRQ-exhaustion logging companion is packaged separately.

Magnus Lindholm:
  sparc64: capture secondary CPUs before normal firmware reboot
  sparc64: advance the PROM console buffer by bytes written
  sparc64: stop PROM console writes when firmware makes no progress
  sparc64: use the kernel log for runtime-capable fatal diagnostics
  sparc: avoid waiting for the PROM output buffer during panic
  sparc64: move panic recovery reporting into architecture code
  sparc64: cache the firmware input node during console discovery
  sparc64: provide status from the direct firmware interface
  sparc64: check client-interface status in PROM wrappers

